     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="method-section reveal" id="does-awst-observe-dst">
  <div class="method-intro">
    <div class="eyebrow">DST Rules</div>
    <h2>Does AWST Observe Daylight Saving Time?</h2>
    <p>
      No, AWST does not observe Daylight Saving Time. Western Australia keeps its clocks at the UTC+8 standard throughout the year.
    </p>
    <p>
      This simplifies scheduling for international organizations, as they do not need to account for spring or autumn transitions in Western Australia.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     9. WHY WESTERN AUSTRALIA DOES NOT CHANGE CLOCKS
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="method-section reveal" id="why-no-dst">
  <div class="method-intro">
    <div class="eyebrow">Political Context</div>
    <h2>Why Western Australia Does Not Change Clocks</h2>
    <p>
      Western Australia has held four referendums on Daylight Saving Time (in 1975, 1984, 1992, and 2009). In each vote, the public rejected the change.
    </p>
    <p>
      Regional and agricultural sectors prefer standard morning daylight hours, and the state's proximity to the equator reduces the need for seasonal adjustments.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

